Package: Pando
Version: 2.0.10
Type: Package
Title: Inferring regulomes from multi-modal single-cell genomics data
Authors@R: person(given = "Jonas Simon",
               family = "Fleck",
               role = c("aut", "cre"),
               email = "jonas.fleck@bsse.ethz.ch",
               comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-4686-7254"))
Maintainer: Jonas Simon Fleck <jonas.fleck@bsse.ethz.ch>
Description: Pando jointly uses scRNA-seq and scATAC-seq measurements to infer
    regulatory relationships between TF-binding site pairs and target genes.
    For two or more conditions within a broad cell type, infer_condition_grn
    uses a statistically screened common-dictionary multi-task procedure.
    Candidate edges are discovered on the complete cell type and separately in
    every condition using the original Pando peak-to-gene, motif,
    peak-target-correlation and TF-target-correlation steps, and exact
    TF-peak-target triples are unioned into a broad candidate dictionary. A
    preliminary joint multi-task ridge fit is used to compute the only
    condition-wise coefficient ridge-Wald P values and BH-adjusted P values in
    the condition workflow. Edges with preliminary BH support in at least one
    condition are unioned to form the final shared fit dictionary, while the
    condition-specific preliminary support mask is retained. The default
    adjusted-P threshold is 0.05 and remains user-configurable over the ordinary
    adjusted-P threshold domain (0, 1), without an additional condition-specific
    upper cap. All conditions are then refitted jointly on this same screened
    dictionary with coefficient inference disabled. The final refit supplies
    quantitative condition coefficients on one common predictor space and does
    not calculate a second coefficient standard error, Wald statistic, P value,
    BH adjustment, or contrast P value. A final edge is active for projection
    when it had preliminary BH support in that condition and its final refit
    coefficient remains estimable and finite. TF-by-ATAC predictors use one
    common equal-condition within-condition RMS scale, matching the
    condition-specific intercepts and equal-condition loss while preventing
    between-condition mean shifts from changing the ridge penalty. Exported
    coefficients are back-transformed to the original TF-by-ATAC units for
    downstream projection. A positive ridge term makes the fitted system
    identifiable under collinearity or raw rank deficiency, and a symmetric
    fusion penalty shrinks condition coefficients toward one another without
    forcing equality. Each fitted condition receives equal total loss weight so
    large conditions do not dominate the joint coefficient solution. One
    target-specific lambda is selected for all conditions by
    condition-stratified cross-validation, using the one-standard-error rule by
    default. Raw design rank, condition-specific zero variance, regularized
    condition number and effective degrees of freedom remain available as
    diagnostics. The public target-condition rsq field uses
    dictionary-conditional out-of-fold R-squared, while in-sample R-squared is
    retained separately. Final pairwise condition contrasts are direct
    differences between the jointly refitted coefficients and are quantitative
    estimates only. Preliminary screen P values remain approximate and
    conditional on data-dependent candidate discovery, CV-selected lambda and
    fusion regularization; they are not P values for the post-screen final
    coefficients. Standard infer_grn retains the original Gaussian GLM as its
    default method and additionally supports a single-task ridge method. Standard
    ridge is the K=1 special case of the same condition ridge solver, so the
    fusion term is exactly zero while the same CV lambda selection, within-task
    RMS predictor scaling, effective degrees of freedom and ridge-Wald
    diagnostics are retained. Condition and standard ridge routing are
    implemented directly in their canonical S3 methods rather than late-loaded
    method replacements. Target-parallel candidate discovery, fixed-dictionary
    GLM fitting and ridge fits dispatch only target-specific RNA, ATAC, motif and
    edge-dictionary slices in worker-sized batches through namespace-level
    workers, avoiding serialization of the mapper frame or the full prepared
    multiome object. BiocParallel target pools are reused across target stages
    and stopped after the corresponding fit when Pando started them. Independent
    cell-type jobs can also be dispatched through BiocParallel, and bundled
    reference datasets are installed as standalone RData files.
